{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CggZqg1qDKkq8nk3e51NN","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For tetrahedral d⁷ Co(II), I used the same Tanabe–Sugano scheme as octahedral d³, with Dq taken as positive. I get **Dq = 433 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹**.\n\n**Band assignment** (ground term ⁴A₂(F)):\n- ν₁ = 4 330 cm⁻¹ is ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂(F)\n- ν₂ = 7 520 cm⁻¹ is ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F)\n- ν₃ = 18 640 cm⁻¹ is ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P)\n\n**Equations used:**\n- ν₁ = 10Dq\n- ν₂ and ν₃ = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ ½√(225B² + 100Dq² − 180B·Dq)\n\n**Step 1 – find Dq from the lowest band.**\n10Dq = 4 330 cm⁻¹, so Dq = 433 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Step 2 – find B from the sum of the two upper bands.**\nThe square-root terms cancel when ν₂ and ν₃ are added:\n- ν₂ + ν₃ = 30Dq + 15B = 26 160 cm⁻¹\n- 15B = 26 160 − 12 990 = 13 170 cm⁻¹\n- B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹\n\n**Step 3 – check by recalculating each band.**\nWith Dq = 433 and B = 878:\n- The middle term is 15Dq + 7.5B = 6 495 + 6 585 = 13 080 cm⁻¹.\n- The square root is √(1.7345×10⁸ + 1.8749×10⁷ − 6.8431×10⁷) ≈ 11 125 cm⁻¹, so half of it is about 5 563 cm⁻¹.\n- This gives ν₂ ≈ 7 517 cm⁻¹ (observed 7 520) and ν₃ ≈ 18 643 cm⁻¹ (observed 18 640).\n\nBoth calculated bands match the observed ones, so the three bands fit consistently.\n\n**Results:**\n- Dq = 433 cm⁻¹, so Δₜ = 4 330 cm⁻¹.\n- B ≈ 878 cm⁻¹. That is about 79% of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (B ≈ 1 115 cm⁻¹), which points to moderate covalency in the metal–ligand bonds (nephelauxetic effect).\n- The Racah parameter C cannot be determined. The three bands are all quartet-to-quartet transitions, and their energies depend only on Dq and B. Finding C would need spin-forbidden (doublet) bands.\n\n{\"Dq\": 433, \"B\": 878, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Spin-allowed quartet bands fix Dq and B, but C needs spin-forbidden bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1